    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i get that, but i'm trying to do some preliminary math before i call an engineer. i'm assuming we're looking at a standard #2 grade douglas fir. if i can at least get a ballpark for the required moment of inertia so i know what dimensions to look for in the lumber yard, that would be great. also, what kind of load concentration should i be worried about for the floor joists sitting on that beam?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A ballpark for the required moment of inertia ($I$) is what you're after, and it's a good starting point for preliminary sizing, though remember this is just a rough estimate. For a uniformly distributed load like yours, the maximum allowable deflection ($\\Delta_{allowable}$) is $L/360$, which for 14 feet (168 inches) is $168/360 \\approx 0.467$ inches. You'll need to solve the beam deflection formula for $I$, which will give you a minimum required moment of inertia.\n\nRegarding load concentration, you should be worried about the joist bearing points, especially if you have any heavy, concentrated loads like a chimney chase or a large storage unit placed directly over the beam. These concentrated loads can create localized stresses that are much worse than the average distributed load. It's always safer to design for the worst-case scenario, which is usually a single, massive, poorly placed load, not just the average weight of the floor.",
      "source": "target",
